# Task 15: Numerical Differentiation

## 1. Objective
The aim is to estimate the derivative of a mathematical function using the central difference method.

## 2. Inputs
The function is f(x) = x^3.
The point of differentiation is x = 2.
The original step size is h = 0.1.

## 3. Mathematical Operations
The central difference formula is:

f'(x) = [f(x+h) - f(x-h)] / 2h

The exact derivative of x^3 at x = 2 is 12.

## 4. Original Output
Step size = 0.1.
Estimated derivative = 12.01.
Absolute error = 0.01.

## 5. Modified Input
The step size was changed from 0.1 to 0.01.

## 6. Observation
The smaller step size produced a more accurate derivative estimate.

## 7. Interpretation
Numerical differentiation estimates the rate of change of a function. A smaller step size can improve accuracy.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem
Numerical differentiation is used in physics and engineering to estimate speed, acceleration and rates of change.

## 9. Assumptions
The function is continuous and differentiable around x = 2.

## 10. New Input Data
The function remains f(x) = x^3.
The point remains x = 2.
The modified step size is h = 0.01.

## 11. Output
Step size = 0.01.
Estimated derivative = 12.0001.
Absolute error = 0.0001.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem
The smaller step size reduced the estimation error from 0.01 to 0.0001, making the result closer to the exact derivative of 12.